### Description

The timing of `System.Drawing.ImageAnimator.Animate` drifts.

### Configuration

* .NET 6.0 (main branch), but it seems to be a longstanding issue since this code has not been changed in a long time.
* Windows 10 and Ubuntu 20.04
* x64
* The behavior is currently different between Windows and Linux, but both present drifts

### Regression?

No

### Other information

Using the animated gifs added to `runtime-assets` in dotnet/runtime-assets#137, and the repro app below, I captured snapshots of animated gifs as each frame is animated. The animated gifs show timers, and the captured rendering shows the animation drifting.

### Repro App

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Drawing;
using System.Drawing.Imaging;
using System.IO;
using System.Threading;

namespace animatorapp
{
    class Program
    {
        static DateTime StartTime;
        static string OutputFolder;
        static Bitmap GIF;
        static List<(string Timestamp, Image Thumbnail)> Snapshots = new();

        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            if (args.Length < 2 || !File.Exists(args[0]))
            {
                throw new ArgumentException("Syntax: appanimator imageFile outputFolder [seconds]. " + string.Join(",", args));
            }

            int seconds = args.Length >= 3 ? int.Parse(args[2]) : 10;
            GIF = new(args[0]);

            StartTime = DateTime.Now;
            SaveSnapshot(GIF);

            ImageAnimator.Animate(GIF, new EventHandler(OnAnimated));

            Thread.Sleep(seconds * 1000);

            ImageAnimator.StopAnimate(GIF, OnAnimated);

            OutputFolder = Path.Combine(args[1], StartTime.ToString("yyyy-MM-dd-HH-mm"));
            Directory.CreateDirectory(OutputFolder);

            foreach (var (timestamp, snapshot) in Snapshots)
            {
                snapshot.Save(Path.Combine(OutputFolder, $"{timestamp}.png"), ImageFormat.Png);
            }
        }

        private static void SaveSnapshot(Image image)
        {
            Snapshots.Add(((DateTime.Now - StartTime).ToString(@"ss\.ff"), image.GetThumbnailImage(128, 128, null, IntPtr.Zero)));
        }

        private static void OnAnimated(object o, EventArgs e)
        {
            Bitmap image = (Bitmap)o;
            ImageAnimator.UpdateFrames(image);
            SaveSnapshot(image);
        }
    }
}
```

### Expected Result

The expected result of this repro app is that the captured thumbnails are generally aligned to the natural timing of the animation, without presenting a drift over time.

### Actual Result

The shorter the delays between the frames in the animation, the more pronounced the drift. For example, a ~100fps animation shows much more drift than a ~1fps animation.

